ections come from the field  of hiv  where the first clinical trial to show  protection from the acquisition of hiv  also came up with the first immune  correlates with protection  and those correlates were not  neutralizing antibodies  nor were they cellular responses  um what protection did correlate with  were high titers of non-neutralizing  antibodies particularly against  the v1 v2 region of the hiv envelope so  the crown region of hiv omf  and these non-neutralizing antibodies  seem to mediate  higher adcc responses against the virus  so further evidence of fc receptor  involvement in the individual control of  hiv comes from  my own work in at mount sinai where we  examined  um a cohort of byremic controllers that  had viral loads below a thousand to  undetectable levels  in comparison to hiv chronically  infected patients  and what we observed was that while  environment controllers and chronically  infected individuals had similar  anti-hiv envelope levels  and also similar levels of  neutralization activity  what we did find was that they had that  hiv controllers had an enhanced capacity  to mediate  fc gamma 2 and fc gamma 3 signaling  so what this is showing is that  while the quantitative aspects of  antibody responses in both these  populations are fairly similar meaning  that the titers are very similar  um the qualitative responses seem to be  different  in that since hiv controllers mediate  higher levels of exchange receptor  signaling  this potentially implies that um they're  mediating  um this is contributing to more  effective immune responses in vivo  so further evidence of the involvement  of fc receptor  involvement in hiv comes from studies  examining broadly neutralizing  antibodies  where in this case the broadly  neutralizing b12  had the uh had its fc region ablated  and then this antibody was passively  infused into macaques  and infected with shiv models of  infection  with a wild type antibody what you see  here is that it maintains both  neutralization  as well as fc receptor function and it  pretty much controls  virus replication in vivo however once  you remove the  fc the ability of this antibody to  interact with fc receptors  you then lose this ability to control  virus replication in vivo  another example of this comes from  the another broadly neutralizing  antibody bnc117  where experiments with this antibody  that also  knock out fc receptor function or  further optimize it to enhance  fc receptor activity  was examined in models of humanized  mouse models of hiv infection  and so here we can see that in  comparison to wild type  the monoclonal antibodies that maintain  neutralizing function but have  their fc of the interaction ability  deleted have higher levels of infection  as compared to wild type  and the converse is true for  antibodies that have enhanced fc  interaction where they have a little bit  less infection as compared to wild type  this is further  corroborated with the viral loads where  the  fc optimized antibody mediates us or  we see significantly lower levels of  viral loads in these animals  so while fc receptor  responses seem to be very uh important  for controlling  virus infections in vivo that's not  always the case in every single type of  virus infection  in fact in dengue infection there are  four serotypes of dengue infection  and what happens is that you get initial  primary infection with one serotype  you then develop an immune response  against this serotype  and then the danger then comes in with  um infection with a secondary uh  serotype of dengue  this reactivates your memory response  but your memory response has been set by  your primary infection  so this leads to an inefficient control  of the secondary serotype infection  this causes elevations in um anti-igg  responses against dengue which  correlates with the severity of disease  and  dengue hemorrhagic fever so um  this caused a initial hypothesis that  maybe the  antibodies themselves were exacerbating  disease  um and studies further showed this um  to be true where in vitro studies have  shown that anti-dengue antibodies  mediate  enhanced entry infection of leukocytes  and this is via  fc receptor mediated uptake further in  vivo studies have shown  that polyclonal serum from severe dengue  and  severely infected dengue individuals or  monoclonal  antibodies mediate pathogenic effects  and further clinical studies have also  shown that  severe dengue is correlated with  enhanced fc gamma 3 signaling in  particular  a particular glycosylation state is  associated with that  moreover uh enhanced polymorphisms that  enhance  fc gamma receptor detection of  antibodies have further been correlated  with disease severity so we can see how  the regulation of sa receptors may be  very important for disease  so we wanted to begin to look at what fc  receptor signaling looked like  in sars kovi ii and so  there have already been uh immune  profiles of mild versus severe patients  and what's been found is that in mild  covid19  what we see is a initial  upregulation of type 1 interferon which  is our body's  way of alerting that there is a viral  infection  occurring and it activates lots of  interferon stimulated genes which act to  counteract virus replication so in  mild disease you have this peak of type  1 interferon  which you have some level of replicating  virus which then  begins to get controlled you have  lower levels of inflammation and this  coincides with  less disease severity  whereas in severe covid19 disease  you have a delay of type 1 interferon  responses  where you have earlier phases of  uncontrolled virus replication  and seven to ten days after the onset of  symptoms  you then get escalating inflammation  and disease severity so what these  inflammation coincides with is what has  been  deemed cytokine storms but really what  this is  is an elevated profile of specific  cytokines  mainly tnf il-6 and il-8  these inflammatory cytokines and this  increased inflammation and disease  severity also coincides  with increasing levels of anti-spike igg  this has led researchers to speculate  that potentially ade  is involved in these responses  so other evidence of potential ade  in coronavirus infections comes from  stars one  and where in vitro studies have shown  that  anti-spike antibodies for against stars  1  mediates higher levels of virus entry  into target cells  this is called antibody dependent  enhanced infection  further evidence comes in vivo where  either through passive infusion of  anti-spike antibodies or vaccine-induced  anti-spike antibodies cause more acute  lung injury and cytokine expression in  macaque models of infection the caveat  to this though  is that in monkey models there is a  resolution of infection  and this resolution of infection or the  decrease in viral titers  coincide with the lung injury  of phenotype and resolving as well  a further caveat to the in vitro data  here is that  while increases a while uh  antibodies increase entry into  macrophages and monocytes the population  thought to  cause the elevated cytokine expression  monocytes and macrophages do not  productively become infected  with sars cov1 and sarsko b2  so to begin to kind of clarify whether  the involvement of fc receptor  signaling in covet 19 we gathered a  cohort of convalescent individuals  these individuals were um antibody  positive or antibody negative  they were 28 antibody positive 28 20  antibody negative  they were sex and age matched fairly  well  importantly the time from which we  there was an onset of symptom in these  patients the time we drew blood  was approximately 43 days that was the  median for the population  and this is very important in that 43  days is optimal  since you don't want to get a too early  of a time point where  igg levels have not peaked yet and you  don't want to get  too late of a time point where igg  levels begin to wane  we've also collected a blood type data  on  all of our donors as well as under any  underlying conditions  in these individuals so the first thing  that we wanted to do was assess their  anti-spike antibody levels and we use  this using a cell-based system  whereby we transfected 293ts with the  sarskovi-2 spike protein  this then expresses the protein on its  surface and allows for  detection by donor-derived igg  this igg was purified igg from these  convalescent donors  we then come in with an anti-human igg  antibody that has a fluorescence marker  on it and we can then detect the level  of  of antibodies via fluorescence uh flow  cytometry  so we can see here that we have very  little background on  a negative sample whereas even with  low samples we get good resolution  and detection this is true also for  samples that have intermediate levels of  anti-spike binding as well as  high levels of anti-spike binding  so in you can see here that the  resolution of the assay  is very good where uh the negative  sera is giving us about a two log  difference between  uh the lowest positive sample that we  see here  and we have about a two log resolution  between the  lowest co b2 positive sample  antibodies positive sample and the  highest  we can further see a breakdown of this  here where you see the spectrum of  anti-spike antibody levels in these  convalescent individuals  importantly in these four individuals  with red arrows  they actually tested negative on  commercial tests um  these tests are normally uh these were  elisa based  tests and they actually said that these  individuals were negative  however um we were able to pick them up  with our assay which  is evidence that our assay is very  sensitive even detecting low levels  and since we have a two log difference  between real negative samples and low  level positive samples we're fairly  confident at these results  also these individuals did also test pcr  positive for stars kobe 2.  so we also collected a symptomology  of these i