ibodies um so  this was very nice in a a set of  convalescent individuals however  we wanted to see how this was reflected  in the hospitalized population  and through a collaboration with sarad  mahandra of mount sinai  we were able to get a handful of  hospitalized individuals  we also looked at their level of  anti-spike binding and what you can see  here  is the level of anti-spike binding in  low versus  high individuals and we also see here  what it looks like in a severe igg  individual and you can see how there's a  marked elevation in the level of  anti-spike antibody binding  when we graph our convalescent cohort  in comparison to hospitalized  individuals we see that there's a very  significant elevation in anti-spike  antibodies in hospitalized  severe and hospitalized individuals and  this is almost a log  um a log full  a log higher as compared to the mean of  our convalescent cohort  when we further divide the level of  anti-spike  antibody levels in those with severity  scores below 45  above 45 and compared to the severe and  hospitalized individuals you see this  clear stair step  in the level of anti-spike antibody  so we next having seen this elevation  and anti-spike  even beyond our cohor our convalescent  cohort levels we wanted to see what the  level of fce gamma receptor activation  was in these patients  and what we observed was with fc gamma 2  we did see a trend towards significantly  higher fc gamma 2  and we did see significantly higher fc  gamma 3 activation  so it does look as if both anti-spike  and fc gamma receptor activation  correlates with severity of covid19  so what we have here is we're left with  a question  of are there likely ade effects in  covid19  and what we've observed is an increase  in the titers of anti-spike antibodies  however we've seen an increase in afghan  receptor signaling  but it's nowhere near the full  difference that we observed  in the level of anti-spike antibody  binding so  we think that while these